<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Bavarian Soviet Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1919 CE · capital Munich</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived council republic in Munich in April 1919, crushed by government troops and Freikorps in a wave of violence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the murder of Bavarian premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-eisner">Kurt Eisner</a> in February 1919, the elected government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-hoffmann">Johannes Hoffmann</a> fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>. On 6-7 April 1919 a council republic was proclaimed by writers and anarchists, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-toller">Ernst Toller</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-muhsam">Erich Mühsam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-landauer">Gustav Landauer</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The first council government was chaotic; within a week the Communists under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugen-levine">Eugen Leviné</a> took over and formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. They decreed the socialisation of banks and industry and seized hostages. Supplies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> broke down. The killing of hostages at the Luitpold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a> on 30 April inflamed opinion against the regime.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Government troops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> units entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 1-3 May 1919, killing several hundred people, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-landauer">Landauer</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugen-levine">Leviné</a> was executed. The episode pushed Bavarian politics sharply to the right.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bavarian-soviet-republic.jpg" alt="Bavarian Soviet Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: R-41 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socialist_red_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Munich</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ernst Toller, Eugen Leviné</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Bavarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bavarian Council Republic, Münchner Räterepublik</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1919&amp;focus=bavarian-soviet-republic" aria-label="Bavarian Soviet Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4949 493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bavarian Soviet Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5269 749L5269 749M5269 749L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5269 749h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Munich. <a href="/atlas?year=1919&amp;focus=bavarian-soviet-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
